how do you define a function in bash:
In Bash you define a function with either of two syntaxes: 

name() { commands; } 
or 
function name { commands; }

Inside a function use positional parameters ($1, $@), set local variables with local var=value, and return an exit status with return N (or output data with echo). Example: myfunc() { local x="$1"; echo "got:$x"; return 2; } ; call it with myfunc hello; check "$?" for the numeric status. Verify definition with declare -f myfunc or type myfunc, and list all functions with declare -F. Functions inherit the caller’s environment unless variables are declared local.

how do you define source ip based matchi:
Source-IP-based matching in Terraform is handled through Cloud Armor's Network Firewall Rules and WAF configurations. To implement this, I define a network firewall rule that matches incoming traffic from specific IP addresses. For example:
```terraform
resource "google_compute_network_firewall_rule" "example" {
  name = "allow-example-ip"
  network = google_compute_network.example.self_link
  source_ranges = ["8.8.8.0/24"]
  allow {
    protocol = "tcp"
    ports    = ["80", "443"]
  }
}
```
I validate this configuration using `terraform fmt` and `terraform validate`. I also monitor the rule's effectiveness by tracking metrics such as request latency and error rates. In case of a failure, I can roll back to a previous version using Terraform's state management features.

how would you create 10 ec2 instances us:
Create an aws_instance resource with count = 10, reference a real AMI and key pair, then run Terraform lifecycle commands. Example HCL:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = data.aws_ami.ubuntu.id
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
  key_name      = "my-key"
  subnet_id     = aws_subnet.app_subnet.id
  count         = 10
  tags = { Name = "web-${count.index}" }
}

data "aws_ami" "ubuntu" { most_recent = true; owners = ["099720109477"]; filter { name = "name"; values = ["ubuntu/images/hvm-ssd/ubuntu-focal-20.04-amd64-server-*"] } }

Run: terraform init; terraform plan; terraform apply --auto-approve. Verify with terraform state list and aws ec2 describe-instances --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=web-*" --query "Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId".

if you use modules how do you pass a val:
Declare an output in the producing module and expose it at the root, then feed that value into the consuming module via its input variable. Example in root module:
module "producer" { source = "./producer" }
module "consumer" { source = "./consumer" some_input = module.producer.some_output }
In producer: output "some_output" { value = aws_instance.example.id } and in consumer declare variable "some_input". Verify with terraform plan (the plan shows the expression), terraform console (e.g., module.producer.some_output), or terraform show -json <planfile> to inspect computed values. For separate state files use the terraform_remote_state data source to read outputs from another state.

what is the parameters block:
In Jenkins Declarative Pipeline the parameters block is a top-level directive that declares build parameters (string, booleanParam, choice, password, etc.) which render on the job’s build form and create entries in the params map at runtime. Example:

pipeline {
  agent any
  parameters {
    string(name: 'BRANCH', defaultValue: 'main', description: 'Git branch')
    booleanParam(name: 'RUN_INTEGRATION', defaultValue: false, description: 'Run integration tests')
  }
  stages { ... }
}

At runtime you access values as params.BRANCH or params.RUN_INTEGRATION. For scripted pipelines use properties([parameters(...)]). You can trigger a parameterized job remotely via JENKINS_URL/job/JOB_NAME/buildWithParameters with --data-urlencode "BRANCH=develop" and verify by echoing params in a stage.

how do you run stages in parallel:
In Declarative Pipeline, use a stage with a parallel block:

pipeline {
  agent any
  stages {
    stage('Parallel work') {
      parallel {
        stage('Unit') { steps { sh 'make test' } }
        stage('Integration') { steps { sh 'make integration' } }
      }
    }
  }
}

In Scripted Pipeline use the parallel step with a map (and optional failFast):

parallel(
  unit: { node { sh 'make test' } },
  integration: { node { sh 'make integration' } },
  failFast: true
)

Each parallel branch runs on its own executor/agent; verify concurrent execution in the build console (overlapping timestamps) and the Jenkins "Build Executor Status" or Blue Ocean UI showing multiple active executors.

how do you deploy a helm chart through j:
Create a Jenkins pipeline that provides kubeconfig to the agent (Credentials "file" or Kubernetes plugin) and run Helm v3 commands on the agent. Example Declarative snippet:

pipeline {
  agent any
  stages {
    stage('Deploy') {
      steps {
        withCredentials([file(credentialsId: 'kubeconfig', variable: 'KUBECONFIG')]) {
          sh 'kubectl config use-context my-cluster'
          sh 'helm repo add myrepo https://charts.example.com && helm repo update'
          sh 'helm upgrade --install my-release myrepo/my-chart --namespace my-ns --create-namespace --values values.yaml'
          sh 'helm status my-release -n my-ns'
          sh 'kubectl rollout status deployment/my-deployment -n my-ns'
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

Verify with helm list -n my-ns and kubectl get pods -n my-ns to confirm rollout.

what are conditional statements in helm:
In Helm, conditional statements are implemented using the `{{ if }}` syntax, which allows you to execute different blocks of code based on conditions. The basic structure is `{{ if condition }} block1 {{ else }} block2 {{ end }}`. For example, you can use this syntax to render a different value in your chart's template based on whether a variable is true or false.

For instance, in a `values.yaml` file, you might define a variable like this: `image_tag: "latest"` and then use it in your template like this:

```
{{ if .Values.imageTag == "latest" }}
  {{- range .Values.imageTags }}
    <img src="{{ . | url }}"/>
  {{ end }}
{{ else }}
  <img src="old-image-url"/>
{{ end }}
```

This allows you to conditionally render different images based on the value of `image_tag`.

how do you use helm in a ci cd pipeline:
To integrate Helm into a CI/CD pipeline, you can leverage Kubernetes' built-in CI/CD tools, such as Jenkinsfile or GitLab CI/CD. Here's an example using Jenkinsfile:

```groovy
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                sh 'helm3 install --upgrade all'
            }
        }

        stage('Deploy') {
            steps {
                sh 'helm3 upgrade --install myapp /path/to/myapp'
            }
        }
    }
}
```

You can also use Helm's `release` command to manage releases in your CI/CD pipeline. For example, you can create a release using the following command:

```bash
helm3 release create my-release --values values.yaml
```

This will create a new release named "my-release" with the specified values from the `values.yaml` file.

Helm is widely adopted and supported in CI/CD pipelines.

how can iam access be restricted using r:
To restrict IAM access using resource tags, you can create a custom policy that checks for specific tags on resources and denies access if they are not present or have incorrect values. You can use the `customMetadata` field in the IAM policy to specify the required tags.

For example, you can create a custom policy like this:

```
{
  "bindings": [
    {
      "role": "roles/iam.serviceAccountAdmin",
      "members": ["serviceAccount:your-service-account-email"],
      "condition": {
        "customMetadata": {
          "requiredTags": [
            {
              "key": "project-id",
              "value": "[PROJECT_ID]"
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "conditions": []
}
```

This policy will only allow the service account to access resources if they have a `project-id` tag with the value of the project ID.

Verification: You can test this policy by creating a resource with and without the required tag, and then attempting to access it using the service account.

can iam conditions reference resource ta:
Yes, IAM Conditions can reference resource tags. To do this, you would specify the `tags` property in your condition's JSON payload. For example:

```json
{
  "condition": {
    "expression": "resource.tags.environment == 'dev'",
    "title": "Environment",
    "description": "The environment of the resource must be 'dev'"
  }
}
```

In this example, the condition will only evaluate to true if the resource has a tag named `environment` with value `dev`. IAM Conditions can reference any number of tags using the same syntax. This feature allows you to enforce policies based on custom metadata associated with resources in your organization.

how do you route api example com and app:
Create two virtual hosts that match the Host header (or SNI) and proxy to different backends. Example with nginx:

upstream api_backend { server 10.0.0.10:8080; }
upstream app_backend { server 10.0.0.11:8080; }

server {
  listen 443 ssl;
  server_name api.example.com;
  ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/site.pem;
  ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/site.key;
  location / { proxy_pass http://api_backend; }
}

server {
  listen 443 ssl;
  server_name app.example.com;
  ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/site.pem;
  ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/site.key;
  location / { proxy_pass http://app_backend; }
}

Verify with nginx -t && systemctl reload nginx and test: curl --resolve api.example.com:443:1.2.3.4 https://api.example.com/ and curl --resolve app.example.com:443:1.2.3.4 https://app.example.com/ to confirm distinct backends.

how do you route api and web to separate:
Use path-based routing in your edge/proxy config. Example with NGINX:

upstream api_backend { server 10.0.0.11:8080; }
upstream web_backend { server 10.0.0.12:80; }

server {
  listen 80;
  server_name example.com;
  location /api/ {
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_pass http://api_backend/;   # trailing slash strips /api/ prefix
  }
  location /web/ {
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_pass http://web_backend/;   # strips /web/ prefix
  }
}

Validate and apply: nginx -t && systemctl reload nginx. Verify end-to-end with curl -v http://example.com/api/health and curl -v http://example.com/web/, and check upstream service logs and access logs (/var/log/nginx/access.log) to confirm correct backend routing.

how do you redirect one domain to anothe:
Configure an HTTP 3xx redirect at the edge (web server, load balancer, or CDN) and point DNS A/ALIAS/CNAME for the source to that edge. Examples:
- nginx: server { listen 80; server_name old.example.com; return 301 https://new.example.com$request_uri; }
- Apache: VirtualHost *:80; ServerName old.example.com; RedirectPermanent / https://new.example.com/
- HAProxy: http-request redirect location https://new.example.com%[capture.req.uri] code 301 if { hdr(host) -i old.example.com }
- AWS ALB: create a rule with “Redirect” action (HTTP code 301, protocol HTTPS, host new.example.com).
Verify with curl -I http://old.example.com and confirm a 301/302 and Location: https://new.example.com header.

can you rate limit by http header:
Yes. Reverse proxies and API gateways can key rate limits on header values. For example in nginx you can use a header variable in limit_req_zone:

limit_req_zone $http_x_api_key zone=api_keys:10m rate=10r/s;
server { … location / { limit_req zone=api_keys burst=5 nodelay; … } }

Reload with nginx -t && systemctl reload nginx. Test by sending repeated requests with curl -H "X-API-Key: key1" and observe 429 responses and access_log status codes once the rate is exceeded. Caveats: headers can be spoofed if not authenticated or terminated at the edge, and some managed WAFs (e.g., AWS WAF rate-based rules) only support IP-based rate limiting.

a load balancer terraform module current:
Change the hostname input to a list, iterate to create listener rules (or a single rule with multiple host_header values), and update outputs. Example variable and resource pattern:

variable "hostnames" {
  type = list(string)
  validation { condition = length(var.hostnames) > 0 }
}

resource "aws_lb_listener_rule" "host" {
  for_each     = { for idx, h in var.hostnames : idx => h }
  listener_arn = aws_lb_listener.http.arn
  priority     = 100 + each.key
  action { type = "forward" target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.app.arn }
  condition { host_header { values = [each.value] } }
}

Verify with terraform plan/apply and confirm rules via AWS CLI: aws elbv2 describe-rules --listener-arn <listener-arn>.

how do you mask passwords and tokens:
Use ingestion-time redaction and field removal (Logstash/Filebeat → Elasticsearch ingest pipelines). In Logstash use mutate.gsub or ruby to replace token patterns and remove sensitive fields, e.g.
filter {
  mutate { gsub => ["message","(?i)(password=)[^\s]+","\1****"] }
  mutate { remove_field => ["plain_password"] }
}
In Filebeat use processors: decode_json_fields → drop_fields or include_fields, or add a decode + script to redact before shipping. In Elasticsearch create an ingest pipeline that uses the gsub processor to mask and remove to delete fields:
PUT _ingest/pipeline/mask-secrets
{ "processors":[{"gsub":{"field":"message","pattern":"(?i)(password=)[^\\s]+","replacement":"$1****"}},{"remove":{"field":"plain_password"}}]}
Verify with _ingest/pipeline/mask-secrets/_simulate and by searching (_search) to confirm no cleartext tokens are stored.

what is mtta:
MTTA (Mean Time To Acknowledge) is the average time between an alert’s creation and the first human (or automated) acknowledgement. Compute it per alert: ack_latency = acknowledged_at − alert_created_at, then MTTA = sum(ack_latency)/N. Use your incident source timestamps—e.g., PagerDuty’s v2 REST API returns incidents[].acknowledged_at and incidents[].created_at; for Postgres you can run: 
SELECT AVG(EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (acknowledged_at - created_at))) FROM incidents WHERE acknowledged_at IS NOT NULL;
Verify by sampling raw events (alerts API or message timestamps) and cross-check a few incidents’ acknowledged_at values against on-call rotation logs to ensure accuracy.

an embedding model change reduces vector:
Confirm the regression with a validation set (measure recall@10, MRR, or cosine similarity drop). Identify the change (git log / PR). Revert the deploy change: git revert <commit> && git push, then kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-service or kubectl set image deployment/my-service my-service=<previous-image>:<tag>. Re-run re-embedding for your corpus with the prior model (example using OpenAI Python): 
from openai import OpenAI; client=OpenAI(); emb=client.embeddings.create(model="text-embedding-3-small", input=batch) 
and upsert vectors to your index (Pinecone: index.upsert(vectors=...)). Verify by running the same validation queries and comparing recall@10, latency (Prometheus request_latency_seconds), and error rates before closing the incident.

